  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1030 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nWednesday night through Monday:\n\nWhile there remains plenty of uncertainty with this next system\npushing in Wednesday night and through Thanksgiving night,\nmodels have definitely started to align a bit more giving us a\nbit more confidence to the situation. This still heavily\ninvolves the developing upper trough which will become a closed \nupper low drifting SE over the central US. This piece appears to\nbe aligning fairly well in models. However a large part of the \nuncertainty comes from the closed low interaction with another \nsystem in the SE US. Will this just be a shortwave extending \nfrom the main low or will this become a completely separate \nclosed low system. This development or lack thereof, appears to \ndirectly impact the track of the main upper low and where the \nbest forcing ends up in addition to the impact it has on \ntemperatures and thus precip type. \n\nPrecip of some kind is looking more likely for parts of \nsouthern WI, particularly the SE portions of the state. The \nearlier part of this event relies upon the 700mb low lifting \nmoisture far enough north with enough WAA/omega to bring in\nprecip. This period has a decent potential to carry some\nrain/snow mix given the 540 line and the cooler\novernight/morning surface temps further into Thanksgiving day\nrain will become more likely given warming temps in the lowest\nlevels. The models show a bit more disagreement in this area \nuntil things come a bit more into line as the system strengthens\nas it pushes northeast. \n\nThe better chances with this system come Thanksgiving late\nafternoon through the night as the surface low develops and \nstrengthens over the northeast US with the 850mb low over the \neastern Great lakes region with a TROWAL region over the the \ncentral Great Lakes region. The TROWAL may allow for precip far \nenough west to impact southern WI. This will most likely remain\nlargely rain based on the 540 line and enough low level WAA. It\nis worth noting that temps will cool overnight and dry out in\nthe lower levels so if moisture holds on long enough aloft some\nwet bulbing may allow for some lighter snow on the backside of\nthe system late Thanksgiving night into Friday morning. \n\nOverall this even should bring mostly rain with a few periods of\nsnow potential with the best snow potential the further west you\ngo on the back edge of the precip shields. \n\nWhile there is more separation and delay in the clipper system\nto follow this weekend, models have definitely kept the system\ndrier over the area later Friday night into Saturday as models\nhave kept the system further north than in previous days runs.\nModels divergence increases significantly into the weekend with\nthis clipper and likely plays a role on the whether a secondary\nsurface low develops in wake of the clipper system. Much of the\nweekend weather will be driven by the speed and development of\nthe upper low pushing through Thanksgiving into Friday. A slower\nprogression, which is more likely with closed low systems, would\nlikely impact the track and development of the upper level\nsystem behind it.\n\nOverall close to normal temperatures are expected with this\nsystem and following it with limited snow potential this weekend \nunless that clipper is able to bring precip further south than \nmodels currently suggest.\n\nKuroski",
